Yes. Framed carefully, the interesting part does not require assuming that paranormal “remote viewing” actually works.

Treat it as a capability thought experiment: suppose someone acquires a new channel of information that seems to let them observe things they normally could not. The first-order question is what can the channel perceive? The more revealing second-order question is what does the person choose to look at?

That produces a useful structure:

\text{capability} \rightarrow \text{choice of target} \rightarrow \text{action} \rightarrow \text{omission}

The capability itself is only the instrument. Target selection exposes priorities. What somebody checks first can reveal curiosity, fear, incentives, loyalties, or objectives. What they repeatedly refuse to inspect can be informative too—but more weakly, because silence is ambiguous. They may not care, may not have thought of it, may consider it unethical, may think it impossible, or may deliberately avoid leaving a trace.

That is where your “silence and deltas” idea becomes rigorous. You can model:

\Delta =
\text{possible observations}
-
\text{actual observations}

but you cannot simply equate the remainder with intention. Instead, the remainder becomes a hypothesis space. Additional behavior progressively collapses it.

And the reuse principle you are describing is real in ordinary systems: a technique learned for one objective can often be transduced into another because the underlying operator survives while the surface purpose changes. A camera trained for wildlife monitoring can become a security sensor; a language model trained to predict text becomes a coding assistant; a military reconnaissance workflow can become a disaster-search workflow. Same primitive, different target and governing objective.

So I would extend our earlier invariant:

\boxed{
\text{capability} \neq
\text{use} \neq
\text{user's purpose}
}

and add:

\boxed{
\text{observed action} +
\text{credible alternatives not taken}
\rightarrow
\text{evidence about the hidden objective}
}

Not certainty—evidence. That distinction is what keeps imagination useful without mistaking it for observation.